Based on the source code, I can explain `filter`'s behavior:

## Truth-Testing

**When predicate is `None`**: The source item itself is truth-tested (line 2924: `O.truth(value)`)

**When predicate is callable**: The result of calling the predicate is truth-tested (line 2924: `O.truth(callObj(this.f, [value]))`)

## Returned Value

The **source item** is always returned, never the predicate result (line 2924: `return value;`)

## Source Exhaustion

When the source iterator is exhausted, `DONE` is returned (line 2923: `if (value === DONE) return DONE;`)

### Source Location

All this behavior is in the `FilterIter.$next()` method at **source/src/runtime/types.ts:2920-2926**:

```typescript
$next(): any {
  for (;;) {
    const value = this.it.$next();
    if (value === DONE) return DONE;
    if (this.f === null ? O.truth(value) : O.truth(callObj(this.f, [value]))) return value;
  }
}
```

The filter iterator is initialized at line 2928 via `iteratorType("filter", FilterIter, (predicate: any, iterable: any) => new FilterIter(predicate, O.iter(iterable)));`

